[asy] advertise
[/asy] Since the volumes of two pyramids must be proportional to the cubes of their corresponding linear dimensions, we can write that as

$$
V_{\text{small}} = (3-4\sqrt{3})^2 V_{\text{large}}
$$

where $V_{\text{large}}$ refers to the volume of the larger pyramid. The volume of any pyramid is given by

$$
V_{\text{pyramid}} = \frac{1}{3} B_h h
$$

where $B_h$ is the area of the base. As long as the area of the base is the same for both problems, we can just write that because one is $8$ ft from the apex:

$$
V_{\text{small}} : V_{\text{large}} = 8^3 : h_{\text{large}}^3
$$

$$
(3-4\sqrt{3})^2 V_{\text{large}} : V_{\text{large}} = 8^3 : h_{\text{large}}^3
$$

$$
(3-4\sqrt{3})^2 = \frac{8^3}{h_{\text{large}}^3}
$$

$$
h_{\text{large}} = 8 (3-4\sqrt{3})
$$

So the larger cross-section is $8 (3-4\sqrt{3})$ ft from the apex of the pyramid.

