Finding the roots of $x^4-4x^3-4x^2+16x-8=0$ is equivalent to factoring the expression. We first factor out an $x^2$ to get $$x^2(x^2-4x-4)+16x-8\\= x^4-4x^3-4x^2+16x-8= x^{2}(x^2-2x-2)+8(x-1)\\= x^{2}(x^2-2x-2)+8(x-1)=x^{2}(x^2-2x-2)+(x-1)(x^2-2x-2)\\=\big{(}x^2+x-1\big{)}(x-1)(x-1)=\big{(}x^2+x-1\big{)}(x-1)^2 \end{align}
By Vieta’s Theorem, the sum of the roots of a polynomial is the negative of the coefficient of the second-to-last term divided by the leading coefficient. Thus, for the polynomial $x^4-4x^3-4x^2+16x-8=0$, we have \begin{align}  -\frac{-4}{1}=-4.\end{align} The answer is $4$. \end{align}

